■■ Operational Requirements
one must have an operational mindset and start with the Concept of Operations (CONOPS) and then delve into how the software will operate for the users

examples:
- cryptographic keys that are shared between applications should be protected and maintained using strict access controls
- data backup and replication must be protected in secure logs with least privilidge implemented
- patching must follow the enterprise process and change to production must be done only after all necessary approvals have been granted

¤¤ Deployment Environment requirements
Some important questions to have answered include:
 Will the software be deployed in an Internet, Extranet or intranet environment?
 Will the software be hosted in a Demilitarized Zone (DMZ)?
 What ports and protocols are available for use?
 What privileges will be allowed in the production environment?
 Will the software be transmitting sensitive or confidential information?
 Will the software be load balanced and how is clustering architected?
 Will the software be deployed in a web farm environment?
 Will the software need to support single sign-on (SSO) authentication?
 Can we leverage existing operating system event logging for auditing purposes?

¤¤ Archiving requirements
x Where will the data or information be stored?
x Will it be in a transactional system that is remote and online or will it be in offline storage media?
x How much space do we need in the archival system?
x How do we ensure that the media is not re-writable? For example, it is better to store archives in Read-Only media instead of Read-Write media.
x How fast will we need to be able to retrieve from archives when
needed? This will not only help in answering the online or offline
storage location question but also help with determining the type
of media to use. For example, for a situation when fast retrieval of
archived data is necessary, archives in tape media is not advisable
because retrieval is sequential and time consuming in tape media.
x How long will we need to store the archives for?
x Is there a regulatory requirement to store the data for a set period of time?
x Is our archival retention policy contradictory to any compliance or regulatory requirements?
x In what format will the data or information be stored? Clear text or cipher text?
x If the data or information is stored in cipher text, how is this
accomplish and are there management processes in place that will
ensure proper retrieval?

¤¤ Anti-piracy requirements
x “The software must be digitally signed to protect against tampering
and reverse engineering.”
x “The code must be obfuscated, if feasible, to deter the duplication
of code.”
x “License keys must not be statically hard-coded in the software
binaries as they can be disclosed by debugging and disassembly.”
x “License verification checks must be dynamic, preferably with
phone-home mechanisms and not be dependent on factors that
the end-user can change.”

